1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,872
SpaceX caught a spaceship.

2
00:00:03,322 --> 00:00:05,600
From orbit, with giant steel arms.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:09,000
On Friday, November thirteenth,

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,346
SpaceX launched Starship Flight
Fifteen from Starbase, Texas.

5
00:00:12,566 --> 00:00:16,195
It was the first flight with a new Block
Three booster and ship flown together.

6
00:00:16,415 --> 00:00:18,841
The ship carried twenty-six
Starlink satellites,

7
00:00:18,841 --> 00:00:20,682
and the rocket was loaded with about

8
00:00:20,682 --> 00:00:23,290
twelve million pounds of
liquid oxygen and methane.

9
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:26,254
The Federal Aviation
Administration approved the launch

10
00:00:26,254 --> 00:00:28,425
about three and a half
hours before liftoff.

11
00:00:28,645 --> 00:00:30,384
About an hour before the launch,

12
00:00:30,384 --> 00:00:33,970
a nationwide ground stop over a
communication issue was announced.

13
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:36,145
Nothing we found says the two are linked.

14
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:41,704
The plan was to catch the Super Heavy booster
with the tower's giant chopstick arms,

15
00:00:41,704 --> 00:00:43,939
about six and a half minutes after launch.

16
00:00:44,159 --> 00:00:47,904
Instead, the booster aborted the catch
and splashed down in the Gulf of Mexico.

17
00:00:48,155 --> 00:00:51,025
First, SpaceX said the system
did an automated abort.

18
00:00:51,245 --> 00:00:55,864
Later, a SpaceX engineer said early signs
point to the boostback burn ending early,

19
00:00:55,864 --> 00:00:57,700
because of a low fuel mass alarm.

20
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,290
The root cause is still
under investigation.

21
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:02,675
The ship kept going.

22
00:01:02,895 --> 00:01:06,386
About twenty-six minutes after
liftoff, its burn put it in orbit,

23
00:01:06,386 --> 00:01:08,964
only the second time
Starship has reached orbit.

24
00:01:09,185 --> 00:01:11,629
It released all twenty-six
Starlink satellites,

25
00:01:11,629 --> 00:01:14,386
the last one at about one
hour and seventeen minutes.

26
00:01:14,635 --> 00:01:18,581
Flight controllers skipped every early
return option, including Hawaii and Chile.

27
00:01:18,801 --> 00:01:22,755
That made this the longest Starship mission
yet, about twenty-two and a half hours.

28
00:01:23,867 --> 00:01:25,034
On Saturday afternoon,

29
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:28,376
the ship fired one engine for
about ten seconds to leave orbit,

30
00:01:28,376 --> 00:01:30,369
then came back through the atmosphere.

31
00:01:30,589 --> 00:01:33,206
At about four twenty in the
afternoon, Eastern time,

32
00:01:33,206 --> 00:01:36,025
the tower's arms caught Ship
Forty-Two on the first try.

33
00:01:36,275 --> 00:01:40,234
It is the first time an orbital Starship
upper stage has ever been recovered.

34
00:01:40,454 --> 00:01:44,223
SpaceX spokesperson Dan Huot put
it this way: we caught a Starship,

35
00:01:44,223 --> 00:01:45,742
and now everything changes.

36
00:01:46,833 --> 00:01:47,975
What comes next?

37
00:01:48,195 --> 00:01:50,681
The cause of the booster
abort is still to come,

38
00:01:50,681 --> 00:01:53,062
and no date has been
given for Flight Sixteen.

39
00:01:53,282 --> 00:01:57,084
SpaceX called the result a step toward
an eventual return to launch site.

40
00:01:57,333 --> 00:01:58,273
Before the flight,

41
00:01:58,273 --> 00:02:00,701
one second-hand estimate
gave a first-try ship

42
00:02:00,701 --> 00:02:02,970
catch at least fifty
or sixty percent odds.

43
00:02:03,190 --> 00:02:06,200
We found no reaction yet
from critics or regulators.

44
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:11,246
Would YOU watch a spaceship get
caught live? Tell me in the comments.

